	<h2>The Mission</h2>
	<p>
		<strong>ErrorLedger</strong> was created to document real-world distributed systems failures, cloud infrastructure incidents, and the architectural decisions behind modern AI platforms. We bypass generic media headlines to dissect the exact technical, physical, and software-driven root causes that trigger major global network failures.
	</p>

	<h2>The Editorial Team</h2>
	<p>
		The <strong>ErrorLedger Editorial Team</strong> consists of independent database developers, Python architects, and infrastructure engineers based in Australia. We have spent years designing, breaking, tuning, and scaling enterprise database systems and local AI tooling frameworks. 
	</p>
	<p>
		We built this platform because modern engineering teams need actionable post-mortems, rigorous documentation, and clear architectural teardowns—not generic SEO summaries. Our goal is to translate real-world disaster scenarios into evergreen architectural lessons, helping systems engineers and software architects build more resilient, self-healing platforms.
	</p>

	<h2>The Standard</h2>
	<p>
		This site is an independent research library built for senior infrastructure teams. Every incident record in our database is compiled using a rigid primary-source authentication hierarchy. We believe that understanding exactly <em>why</em> a network collapsed or a memory buffer overflowed is the most valuable step in preventing the next disaster.
	</p>
	<p>
		For full transparency into how we gather facts, verify claims, and build our technical teardowns, please review our <a href="/methodology">Research Methodology</a>.
	</p>
